{
  "id": "tumba_francesa",
  "name": "Tumba francesa (eastern Cuban Franco-Haitian drum-dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Drum, song and dance societies founded in eastern Cuba in the 19th century by Haitian-born and Franco-Haitian people who arrived with French planters after the Haitian Revolution; the surviving societies are La Caridad de Oriente in Santiago de Cuba, Pompadour Santa Catalina de Riccis in Guantánamo and Bejuco in Sagua de Tánamo, and UNESCO proclaimed the tradition a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity. A lead singer (composé) sings in Creole and Spanish, answered by a chorus, over large hand-played drums (tumbas), the catá, a hollow log beaten with sticks, and the chachá, a metal rattle; masón, yubá and frenté are its main dances. A generic membrane-drum card stands in for the tumbas, the palitos card for the sticks on the catá and a generic shaker card for the chachá.",
  "recipe": "classic modern tumba francesa, groove Percussion, Caribbean branch. membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, hollow toned palitos, shaker source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
